  "overview": {
    "text": "Neutrons are subatomic particles found with protons in atomic nuclei; ordinary hydrogen-1 is the exception, with a nucleus containing one proton and no neutron. A neutron has no net electric charge and is slightly more massive than a proton. Its three valence quarks are one up and two down, bound by the strong interaction; their charges add to zero. Neutron number distinguishes isotopes of the same element, which keep the same proton count but can have different properties: carbon-12 is stable, whereas carbon-14 is radioactive. Neutrons are also useful in research: neutron scattering helps scientists investigate the properties of materials.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsneutrons",
      "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsisotopes",
      "https://www2.lbl.gov/Science-Articles/Research-Review/Magazine/1997-fall/vr/quark.html"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Neutrons have no net electric charge and are slightly more massive than protons.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsneutrons"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A neutron’s three valence quarks are one up quark and two down quarks; their charges combine to make the neutron electrically neutral.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsneutrons",
        "https://www2.lbl.gov/Science-Articles/Research-Review/Magazine/1997-fall/vr/quark.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Isotopes of an element have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsisotopes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Carbon-12 is stable, while carbon-14 is radioactive, illustrating how differing neutron counts can accompany different isotope properties.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsisotopes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Neutron scattering helps researchers investigate material properties, including the shapes and sizes of crystals and molecules.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsneutrons"
      ]
    }
  ],
